# KQL's `innerunique`: DS0124 explains the divergence (D-004). The fix changes meaning, so it is not machine-applicable.
mode: syntax
---- query
'orders.csv' | join kind = innerunique 'customers.csv' on customerId
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..69
  PIPELINE@0..68
    LITERAL@0..12
      STRING@0..12 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@12..13 " "
    PIPE@13..14 "|"
    WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
    JOIN_OP@15..68
      IDENT@15..19 "join"
      WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
      JOIN_KIND@20..38
        IDENT@20..24 "kind"
        WHITESPACE@24..25 " "
        EQ@25..26 "="
        WHITESPACE@26..27 " "
        IDENT@27..38 "innerunique"
      WHITESPACE@38..39 " "
      LITERAL@39..54
        STRING@39..54 "'customers.csv'"
      WHITESPACE@54..55 " "
      IDENT@55..57 "on"
      WHITESPACE@57..58 " "
      NAME_REF@58..68
        IDENT@58..68 "customerId"
  WHITESPACE@68..69 "\n"

error[DS0124]: `innerunique` is not supported
  --> query:1:28
  |
1 | 'orders.csv' | join kind = innerunique 'customers.csv' on customerId
  |                            ^^^^^^^^^^^ this is KQL's default join kind
  |
  = help: use `kind = inner`; to keep one left row per key, as KQL does, remove the duplicates first, for example with `distinct`
  = note: unlike KQL, DataScript never drops duplicate keys from the left side; the default join kind is `inner`
